From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 16:06:39 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: mediatek: mt6797-spm-sleep: program SYS_CIRQ after the
 kick again

0816 moved the SYS_CIRQ programming and the GIC mask to before the PCM
reset, so an interrupt arriving while the program loads would be
recorded. With that order a SODI test period stopped returning after
21 good ones, with an inbound ping (stage D5a), and the device reset.
The same test passed hundreds of periods with the order up to 0806.

Program SYS_CIRQ and mask the GIC after the kick again. Keep the check
for an enabled interrupt already pending, now before the PCM reset with
the GIC unmasked, so such a period is still refused.
---
 drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-spm-sleep.c | 25 +++++++++++--------------
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-spm-sleep.c b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-spm-sleep.c
index 32da1b716..9599a3c0d 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-spm-sleep.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-spm-sleep.c
@@ -758,20 +758,11 @@ static int spm_run(const struct spm_scen *sc)
 		return -EBUSY;
 
 	/*
-	 * SODI: record the device interrupts in SYS_CIRQ and mask them at the
-	 * GIC before the SPM changes, then refuse the period if one is already
-	 * pending. From here on, an interrupt is recorded and ends the period.
+	 * SODI: refuse the period if an enabled interrupt is already pending,
+	 * since SYS_CIRQ, programmed only after the kick, would not see it.
 	 */
-	if (sc->cirq) {
-		spm_cirq_enable();
-		if (gic_mask)
-			spm_gic_mask();
-		if (spm_gic_pending()) {
-			spm_cirq_disable();
-			spm_gic_restore();
-			return -EAGAIN;
-		}
-	}
+	if (sc->cirq && spm_gic_pending())
+		return -EAGAIN;
 
 	/* C1 first: if MCUCFG ignores the writes, stop before the SPM changes */
 	axi[0] = readl(s.mcucfg + MP0_AXI_CONFIG);
@@ -881,8 +872,14 @@ static int spm_run(const struct spm_scen *sc)
 	 * (mt_irq_mask_all), so only the program wakes the CPU. Here CPU0 stays
 	 * powered, and without this a wake interrupt can take it out of WFI at
 	 * the GIC while the program goes on sleeping.
+	 *
+	 * SYS_CIRQ is programmed after the kick, as up to 0806: with it
+	 * programmed before the PCM reset, test periods stopped returning
+	 * (D5a).
 	 */
-	if (!sc->cirq && gic_mask)
+	if (sc->cirq)
+		spm_cirq_enable();
+	if (gic_mask)
 		spm_gic_mask();
 	/* From cpuidle the SPM's interrupt is not enabled in Linux: enable it here */
 	if (sc->idle)
-- 
2.43.0

